### Is there an already existing issue for this?

- [X] I have searched the existing issues

(Closest is #6594, permanent match loss when a TypeLookup reply never arrives. This report is about unsynchronised access to the same bookkeeping, which #6594 doesn't cover.)

### Expected behavior

`TypeLookupManager`'s asynchronous type resolution state is used by two kinds of threads at once: the receive threads that process remote DATA(w)/DATA(r) (EDP listeners → `async_get_type()` → `check_type_identifier_received()`), and the TypeLookup reply processor thread (`dds.tls.replies`, `TypeLookupReplyListener::process_reply()`). `TypeLookupManager.hpp` documents `async_get_types_mutex_` as the mutex protecting `async_get_type_callbacks_` and `async_get_type_requests_`, so every access to `async_get_type_writer_callbacks_`, `async_get_type_reader_callbacks_` and `async_get_type_requests_` is expected to hold it. `request_seq_number_` is likewise expected to be synchronised. A participant discovering remote endpoints of types it doesn't know should resolve them without data races.

### Current behavior

Built with `-fsanitize=thread`, a participant that discovers remote writers of types it doesn't know reports data races in `TypeLookupManager` in every run: 3 out of 3 runs of the reproducer below gave 3–4 reports each, all in the discovering process. The code involved (v3.6.2, byte-identical on `master` @ 24141edc4c) has four unsynchronised accesses:

1. **Callback map insert outside the lock.** `check_type_identifier_received()` takes `async_get_types_mutex_` only around the find/`push_back` of an already pending type ([L391-L404](https://github.com/eProsima/Fast-DDS/blob/v3.6.2/src/cpp/fastdds/builtin/type_lookup_service/TypeLookupManager.cpp#L391-L404)). For a new type it sends the request and then calls `async_get_type_callbacks.emplace(...)` after the lock scope has closed ([L406-L417](https://github.com/eProsima/Fast-DDS/blob/v3.6.2/src/cpp/fastdds/builtin/type_lookup_service/TypeLookupManager.cpp#L406-L417)).
2. **Callback maps read and iterated without the lock.** `notify_callbacks()` runs on the reply thread ([L429-L460](https://github.com/eProsima/Fast-DDS/blob/v3.6.2/src/cpp/fastdds/builtin/type_lookup_service/TypeLookupManager.cpp#L429-L460)). It `find`s in both callback maps and iterates the pending vectors, invoking each callback, without taking `async_get_types_mutex_`. Meanwhile a receive thread may `emplace` into the map (point 1; a rehash invalidates the table being read) or `push_back` into the very vector being iterated (L400, under the lock, which the reader doesn't take; a reallocation invalidates the iterator). This is the race the TSan excerpt below shows.
3. **Request map lookup without the lock.** `TypeLookupReplyListener::process_reply()` `find`s in `async_get_type_requests_` without the lock ([TypeLookupReplyListener.cpp L106](https://github.com/eProsima/Fast-DDS/blob/v3.6.2/src/cpp/fastdds/builtin/type_lookup_service/TypeLookupReplyListener.cpp#L106)). Meanwhile `add_async_get_type_request()` `emplace`s into it, under the lock, from a receive thread ([L469](https://github.com/eProsima/Fast-DDS/blob/v3.6.2/src/cpp/fastdds/builtin/type_lookup_service/TypeLookupManager.cpp#L469)).
4. **Request sequence number unsynchronised.** `create_request()` is `const` and reads and increments the `mutable` `request_seq_number_` with no synchronisation ([L706-L707](https://github.com/eProsima/Fast-DDS/blob/v3.6.2/src/cpp/fastdds/builtin/type_lookup_service/TypeLookupManager.cpp#L706-L707)). It is reached from every receive thread that processes EDP data, so two requests can be given the same sequence number, i.e. the same `SampleIdentity`, and a reply can be matched to the wrong request.

There is also an ordering problem in point 1. The request is sent ([L407-L408](https://github.com/eProsima/Fast-DDS/blob/v3.6.2/src/cpp/fastdds/builtin/type_lookup_service/TypeLookupManager.cpp#L407-L408)) and recorded in `async_get_type_requests_` (L412) *before* the callback entry is stored (L417). A reply processed in that window finds no callbacks in `notify_callbacks()`, so the endpoints waiting on that type are never notified. This is one more way into the permanent match loss described in #6594.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Build Fast DDS (and Fast-CDR) with `-fsanitize=thread`.
2. Build the two-process reproducer below against it:
   `g++ -std=c++17 -g -O1 -fsanitize=thread typelookup_race.cpp -o typelookup_race -lfastdds -lfastcdr`
3. On one host, start the process that only discovers, then the one that announces 200 writers, each of its own dynamic type:
   ```
   export TSAN_OPTIONS="log_path=$PWD/tsan halt_on_error=0"
   setarch $(uname -m) -R ./typelookup_race discover &
   sleep 2
   setarch $(uname -m) -R ./typelookup_race announce 200
   wait
   ```
   (`setarch -R` is only needed on kernels where TSan fails to map its shadow memory with ASLR on.)
4. `tsan.<pid of discover>` holds the data race reports (3–4 per run here). The announcing process reports nothing.

<details><summary>typelookup_race.cpp</summary>

```cpp
// Two-process reproducer for an unsynchronised access to TypeLookupManager's async_get_type callback maps.
//
//   typelookup_race announce <count>  creates <count> DataWriters, each of its own dynamic struct type, so every
//                                     writer is announced with TypeInformation of a type no other process knows
//   typelookup_race discover          creates a DomainParticipant and nothing else: discovering the announcing
//                                     process's writers has it resolve each type over the TypeLookup service
//
// Run both on one host, discover first, against a Fast DDS built with -fsanitize=thread.
#include <fastdds/dds/domain/DomainParticipant.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/domain/DomainParticipantFactory.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/publisher/DataWriter.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/publisher/Publisher.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/topic/Topic.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/topic/TypeSupport.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/xtypes/dynamic_types/DynamicPubSubType.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/xtypes/dynamic_types/DynamicTypeBuilderFactory.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/xtypes/dynamic_types/MemberDescriptor.hpp>
#include <fastdds/dds/xtypes/dynamic_types/TypeDescriptor.hpp>

#include <chrono>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <thread>

using namespace eprosima::fastdds::dds;

namespace
{
    constexpr DomainId_t domain = 42;
    constexpr auto run_time = std::chrono::seconds{15};

    DynamicType::_ref_type make_type(int index)
    {
        TypeDescriptor::_ref_type type_descriptor{traits<TypeDescriptor>::make_shared()};
        type_descriptor->kind(TK_STRUCTURE);
        type_descriptor->name("RaceType" + std::to_string(index));
        DynamicTypeBuilder::_ref_type builder{
            DynamicTypeBuilderFactory::get_instance()->create_type(type_descriptor)};
        MemberDescriptor::_ref_type member{traits<MemberDescriptor>::make_shared()};
        member->name("value" + std::to_string(index));
        member->type(DynamicTypeBuilderFactory::get_instance()->get_primitive_type(TK_INT32));
        builder->add_member(member);
        return builder->build();
    }
} // namespace

int main(int argc, char **argv)
{
    const std::string mode = argc > 1 ? argv[1] : "";
    DomainParticipant *participant =
        DomainParticipantFactory::get_instance()->create_participant(domain, PARTICIPANT_QOS_DEFAULT);
    if (participant == nullptr)
    {
        std::cerr << "no participant\n";
        return 1;
    }

    if (mode == "announce")
    {
        const int count = argc > 2 ? std::atoi(argv[2]) : 100;
        Publisher *publisher = participant->create_publisher(PUBLISHER_QOS_DEFAULT);
        for (int index = 0; index < count; ++index)
        {
            TypeSupport type{new DynamicPubSubType(make_type(index))};
            type.register_type(participant);
            Topic *topic = participant->create_topic("race_topic_" + std::to_string(index), type.get_type_name(),
                                                     TOPIC_QOS_DEFAULT);
            publisher->create_datawriter(topic, DATAWRITER_QOS_DEFAULT);
        }
        std::cout << "announcing " << count << " writers" << std::endl;
    }
    else if (mode != "discover")
    {
        std::cerr << "usage: typelookup_race announce <count> | discover\n";
        return 2;
    }

    std::this_thread::sleep_for(run_time);
    participant->delete_contained_entities();
    DomainParticipantFactory::get_instance()->delete_participant(participant);
    return 0;
}
```

</details>

### Fast DDS version/commit

v3.6.2 (tag, 39303846fb). The racing files, `TypeLookupManager.cpp` and `TypeLookupReplyListener.cpp`, are byte-identical on `master` @ 24141edc4c (2026-10-29). The build we observed this on carries downstream patches in unrelated files; there are none under `src/cpp/fastdds/builtin/type_lookup_service/`.

### Platform/Architecture

Other. Please specify in Additional context section.

### Transport layer

Default configuration, UDPv4 & SHM

### Additional context

- Ubuntu 24.04.5 LTS, amd64, GCC 13.3.0, `-fsanitize=thread`.
- First seen running eProsima DDS Record & Replay's `ddsrecorder` (v1.5.x) under ThreadSanitizer. It resolves every type it discovers through the TypeLookup service and reports the same races; the reproducer above isolates them to plain Fast DDS.

Possible fix:
- In `check_type_identifier_received()`, hold `async_get_types_mutex_` across the find-or-insert. Store the callback entry and the request record *before* sending the request, so no reply can be processed before both exist, and remove them again if sending fails.
- In `notify_callbacks()`, move the pending entries out of both maps under the lock, then invoke the callbacks and delete the proxies outside it.
- In `process_reply()`, look up and remove the outstanding request under the lock.
- Make `request_seq_number_` atomic, or take it under the lock.

### XML configuration file

None (default QoS and profiles).

